"\n## Linux DRM - GEM: handle, dumb buffer, mmap\n\n### Introduction\n| Part | Topic |\n|---|---|\n| Part 1 | Architecture and the Big Picture |\n| Part 2 | Core Initialization and Driver Registration |\n| Part 3 | open() and ioctl() Code Flow |\n\nIn Parts 1 through 3, we examined the DRM architecture and the open/ioctl flow, noting that object_idr inside struct drm_file serves as the GEM handle table. This post begins from there.  \n\n| Question | Code Examined in This Post |\n|---|---|\n| Who creates and references the buffers used by the GPU? | GEM (drm_gem_object, handle) |\n| How is the simplest buffer created? | Dumb buffer creation (DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB) |\n| How does the CPU read and write to that buffer? | mmap, fake offset, page fault |\n\n\n\n\n### Why Is Memory Management DRM's Job\nBecause the GPU is a separate processor from the CPU, rendering graphics requires both the CPU and GPU to manage the same memory together. Textures, framebuffers, and command buffers all fall into this category. This presents challenges that cannot be solved with standard malloc.  \n\n- To allow GPU access, memory must be pinned to physical pages and mapped into a GPU-accessible address space (via an IOMMU or the GPU's own page table).\n- When a process terminates, the GPU memory it occupied must be automatically reclaimed.\n- Different processes must view the same buffer, such as a display server and a client application.\n- Synchronization is required so that the CPU does not modify a buffer while the GPU is actively accessing it.\n\nDRM solves this problem through an interface called GEM (Graphics Execution Manager). It represents every GPU buffer as a single struct drm_gem_object, while a backend (helper) determines where the actual memory is sourced. Representative examples include drm_gem_shmem_helper.c, which uses main memory shmem; drm_gem_dma_helper.c, which uses contiguous memory; and TTM-based drm_gem_ttm_helper.c, which manages dedicated GPU memory (VRAM) as well. The code in this post follows the simplest shmem backend (used by drivers like vkms).  \n\n### The Core of GEM: drm_gem_object and Handles\n#### The Object: struct drm_gem_object\n\n\n> **Source** include/drm/drm_gem.h L280-L455 (Linux v6.19)\n\n- funcs: The function table implemented by the backend (free, mmap, vmap, export, get_sg_table, ...). The core handles common tasks and delegates memory-type-specific logic here.\n- filp: In the shmem backend, this is the shmem file holding the actual backing memory of the object.\n- vma_node: Holds the \"fake offset\" used to locate the object during mmap (covered in the mmap section below).\n- dma_buf, import_attach: The dma-buf connection used when sharing buffers with other devices (covered in the PRIME section).\n- resv: The dma_resv that aggregates fences attached to this buffer (covered in the synchronization section).\n\n\n\n> **Source** include/drm/drm_gem.h L72-L235 (Linux v6.19)\n\n#### The Name Tag: Handles Are per fd\nUserspace cannot directly know the object's kernel pointer. Instead, it receives an integer handle. This handle is an index into drm_file.object_idr introduced in Part 1. In other words, handles reside in an independent namespace for each open()ed file descriptor (fd); even for the exact same object, different processes can have different handles.  \n\n\nThis table is initialized during open() (in drm_file_alloc, covered in Part 3). This is also where handle numbering is configured to start from 1.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1027-L1032 (Linux v6.19)\n\nThe function that creates handles is drm_gem_handle_create(), while the actual work is performed by its tail function.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L437-L495 (Linux v6.19)\n\n- idr_alloc(&file_priv->object_idr, NULL, 1, 0, ...): Allocates an available number >= 1 from this file's IDR table. Initially, NULL is placed instead of the object pointer.\n- drm_vma_node_allow(): Grants this file permission to mmap this object (checked later via drm_vma_node_is_allowed).\n- obj->funcs->open(): Backend callback.\n- Finally, idr_replace() swaps NULL with the actual object pointer. This sequence prevents concurrent threads from looking up a half-initialized handle.\n\n### Code Flow 1: Creating a Dumb Buffer\nLet us trace GEM through the simplest buffer creation path: the dumb buffer. This is the most basic buffer used for software rendering on screen; it lacks GPU acceleration features and is essentially \"flat memory that the CPU can paint into.\" Userspace creates one using a single ioctl: DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB.  \n\n\nCalculating and validating the buffer size using width, height, and bpp passed by userspace is the core's job.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_dumb_buffers.c L191-L228 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndev->driver->dumb_create is the driver callback. As seen in Part 2, the DRM_GEM_SHMEM_DRIVER_OPS macro in vkms's drm_driver populates this callback with drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** include/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.h L302-L304 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L558-L568 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L470-L491 (Linux v6.19)\n\nThe operational sequence unfolds as follows:  \n\n1. drm_gem_shmem_create() creates the object with an initial reference count of 1.\n2. drm_gem_handle_create() creates the handle. Internally, drm_gem_object_handle_get() increments handle_count, taking an additional object reference only when it is the first handle (if (obj->handle_count++ == 0) drm_gem_object_get(obj)). Subsequent handles simply increment the counter.\n3. Finally, drm_gem_object_put() drops the initial reference (source comment: \"handle holds it now\"). The object's lifecycle is now tied to the handle. When userspace closes the handle, or when the file is closed, drm_gem_release() releases all remaining handles in that file's object_idr, freeing the object.\n\nCleanup upon file closure is just as straightforward:  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1043-L1049 (Linux v6.19)\n\nEven if a process crashes unexpectedly, the kernel invokes this function while closing the fd, preventing GPU memory leaks. Now let us turn back to __drm_gem_shmem_init(), which creates the object and performs two critical tasks.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L52-L94 (Linux v6.19)\n\n- drm_gem_object_init_with_mnt() creates a shmem file using shmem_file_setup(\"drm mm object\", size, ...). Each object is essentially an anonymous memory file with a fixed size.\n- drm_gem_create_mmap_offset() pre-allocates and reserves the fake mmap offset for this object (covered in the mmap section below).\n\nAt this stage, no physical pages have been allocated yet. Pages are allocated on demand later inside drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_locked().  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L230-L262 (Linux v6.19)\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L623-L688 (Linux v6.19)\n\nshmem_read_folio_gfp() reads pages from the shmem file (allocating them if not already present) into an array, and mapping_set_unevictable() pins them to prevent swapping. This is the concrete code implementing the principle stated earlier: \"to be used by a GPU, memory must be pinned to physical pages.\"  \n\n### Code Flow 2: Painting from the CPU with mmap\nAlthough the buffer has been created, the CPU cannot access it yet. Because multiple buffers reside behind the single /dev/dri/card0 file descriptor, calling mmap(fd, ...) alone does not identify which buffer to map. DRM solves this using a fake offset, repurposing the file offset (the final argument to mmap) as an object identifier.  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n#### Step 1: Getting the Offset (MAP_DUMB)\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L395-L420 (Linux v6.19)\n\ndrm_gem_object_lookup() looks up the object using the handle (object_idr query) and returns the address of the reserved vma_node as the offset. This offset is not an actual file offset, but a fake address managed per device by drm_vma_offset_manager.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L557-L564 (Linux v6.19)\n\n#### Step 2: mmap()\nThe mmap() syscall enters via .mmap = drm_gem_mmap in DRM_GEM_FOPS, which we explored in Part 3.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1205-L1251 (Linux v6.19)\n\n- It finds the vma_node using vma->vm_pgoff (= offset converted to page count) and retrieves drm_gem_object via container_of.\n- drm_vma_node_is_allowed(node, priv): Only files previously authorized by drm_vma_node_allow() are allowed through. This safeguard prevents other processes from mapping someone else's buffer simply by guessing the offset value.\n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1146-L1187 (Linux v6.19)\n\nThis links the object's funcs->mmap and vm_ops. The shmem backend uses this mmap call to actually acquire pages (get_pages) and configure the mapping with VM_PFNMAP.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L653-L691 (Linux v6.19)\n\nWhen userspace first reads or writes to the mapped address, a page fault occurs, and the object's fault handler maps the backing pages into the page table entries one by one.  \n\n\n\n> **Source** drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L571-L599 (Linux v6.19)\n\nshmem->pages[page_offset] is the array collected earlier by drm_gem_get_pages(), and vmf_insert_pfn() maps that page directly into the user's virtual address space. Through this mechanism, a single user line such as *(uint32_t *)(px + ...) = color writes directly into the shmem pages backing the GEM object.  \n\n### Code Locations\n| Stage | Function | Location (Linux v6.19) |\n|---|---|---|\n| GEM: handle table initialization | drm_gem_open() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:1027 |\n| GEM: handle creation (idr_alloc) | drm_gem_handle_create_tail() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:437 |\n| GEM: object lookup | drm_gem_object_lookup() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:822 |\n| dumb: size validation and dumb_create invocation | drm_mode_create_dumb() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_dumb_buffers.c:191 |\n| dumb: shmem backend creation | drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c:558 |\n| dumb: creation and handle association | drm_gem_shmem_create_with_handle() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c:470 |\n| dumb: shmem file creation, mmap offset reservation | __drm_gem_shmem_init() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c:52 |\n| dumb: page acquisition | drm_gem_get_pages() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:623 |\n| mmap: returning offset | drm_gem_dumb_map_offset() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:395 |\n| mmap: entry | drm_gem_mmap() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:1205 |\n| mmap: linking vm_ops | drm_gem_mmap_obj() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c:1146 |\n| mmap: page fault | drm_gem_shmem_fault() | drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c:571 |\n\n\n\n\n### Summary\nIn this post, we have explored the following:  \n\n- GPU buffers are represented by a single GEM object, which userspace references via an independent handle per fd (drm_file). The handle's lifetime governs the object's lifetime.\n- Dumb buffers are implemented through a shmem file + mmap fake offset, with physical pages allocated on demand when first needed.\n- mmap resolves the object via the fake offset and maps backing pages one by one upon a page fault. drm_vma_node_is_allowed() prevents unauthorized processes from mapping arbitrary buffers by simply knowing their offset.\n\nHandles are meaningful only within their originating drm_file. Next, we will explore how to share these buffers with other processes or hardware devices using PRIME and dma-buf."